{"id": "striviaqa0069", "pool": "striviaqa", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 1783403249, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "81e2f23f98f19f1f63c3360e6be048d11083a3634e93068c14d5fdb8186e6ebb", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 3, "query": "In Greek mythology, which virgin goddess of the hearth, home and cooking gave up her seat as one of the Twelve Olympians to tend to the sacred flame on Mount Olympus, for Dionysus?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/striviaqa0069.wav", "input_sha256": "ece97b07de142e6992e475bb0ed731127a0ad02b0a7cdcd2e71996be85e90a17", "input_samples": 154093, "input_s": 9.631, "n_real_chunks": 10, "score": 0.4831, "act": 0.9999, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "That would be H estia. She is the god dess of the hearth, home, and cooking, and she was known for her quiet and humble nature. In many accounts, when the twelve Olympians were chosen after the war among the gods, He stia offered to give up her seat so that Dionys us could join the divine council on Mount Oly mpus. So although she remained an important deity, she stepped aside out of respect or perhaps even affection for the new god of wine and ritual.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 10, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 27, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/striviaqa/audio/striviaqa0069_local_local.wav", "sha256": "970cf480cc3bcadb3b5900dfcb94b15ec3bda48c7398f0209968b9c223fa1099", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 26.84}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.076, 1.139, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.078, 2.14, 1, 